    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Always verify whether a body is Constitutional, Statutory, or Executive, as this is a frequent source of preliminary exam trap questions.",
        "Expenses of major constitutional posts are charged on the Consolidated Fund of India, meaning they cannot be subjected to a parliamentary vote.",
        "NITI Aayog and the National Development Council are classic examples of non-constitutional, extra-statutory executive bodies."
      ],
      "id": "2ra3jsd"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Constitutional bodies derive their powers, status, and authority directly from specific articles of the Constitution of India.",
        "Statutory bodies are established by an Act passed by Parliament or a State Legislature and can be modified or abolished by simple legislation.",
        "Executive bodies are created through an executive resolution of the Cabinet and possess neither constitutional nor statutory backing.",
        "Expenses and salaries of key constitutional bodies are charged upon the Consolidated Fund of India and are non-votable by Parliament."
      ],
      "id": "8iezywi"
    }
